    "narrative": "The LawDog lineup is anchored today by **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, who boasts the day\u2019s highest efficiency score at 72.72. Given his elite profile, he is an auto-start despite the \"Lineup Pending\" tag. Joining him at the top of the recommendation list is **Lawrence Butler** (72.49) and **Tyler Freeman** (70.18). **Lawrence Butler** benefits immensely from a soft matchup against Jack Kochanowicz, with a +19% bump for SP skill, while **Tyler Freeman** thrives in a high-conversion park environment (+14% park factor) and faces an opposing battery with a high susceptibility to stolen bases (94% SB rate), a critical factor in our strategy to bridge the SB gap.\n\nThe most difficult decision for the rotation today involved **Masyn Winn**, who landed on the bench with a 45.13 score. While **Masyn Winn** remains a roster staple, he was squeezed out by the sheer volume of high-scoring, multi-category contributors like **Will Smith** (69.89) and **Matt Chapman** (64.68). **Matt Chapman** is particularly well-positioned, as his score is bolstered by the 87% SB vulnerability of Merrill Kelly, giving him an edge in our scarcest category. We are monitoring the status of **Will Smith**, **Daulton Varsho**, and **Lawrence Butler** closely; as their official lineups are still pending, any shift down the batting order could trigger a recalibration of their efficiency scores. \n\nRegarding risks, we are proceeding with caution on **Bryson Stott** (50.33) and **Drew Gilbert** (50.19). **Bryson Stott** faces a significant platoon disadvantage against Andrew Abbott (-13%), and while **Drew Gilbert** is a viable play, his position at the bottom of the order (-8%) drags down his floor. We are keeping **Gavin Sheets** and **Jeremiah Jackson** in reserve as high-upside alternatives should late-breaking lineup changes force a pivot. **Cal Raleigh** remains sidelined on the IL, leaving us to navigate today\u2019s matchups with our active depth.",
